WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.420
Level 3 supports site-wide operations and coordination across production areas.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.210
Common functions include operations management, manufacturing execution,

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:12.970
batch or recipe management, site historians, engineering services, OT administration,

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.484
and other systems needed to run the facility as a whole.

00:00:15.484 --> 00:00:19.970
A historian may collect process data from several areas for trending, quality analysis,

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.349
reporting, and investigation.

00:00:21.349 --> 00:00:24.890
A manufacturing execution system can coordinate work orders, materials,

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.120
and production status.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.465
Batch systems manage recipes and sequences.

00:00:28.465 --> 00:00:32.500
Engineering workstations maintain controller logic and device configurations.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.785
These functions have broader scope than a single HMI or controller.

00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:38.950
Level 3 systems are often conventional servers and workstations,

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.280
but their operational context changes their risk.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.810
An engineering workstation may have authority to modify many controllers.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:48.666
A compromised operations server may disrupt coordination across several lines.

00:00:48.666 --> 00:00:52.580
A historian can become a pathway toward lower levels or a bridge toward enterprise

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.624
consumers if its connections are not controlled.

00:00:54.624 --> 00:00:58.660
Security priorities include privileged-access management, secure administration,

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.385
application allowlisting where appropriate, tested backups, patch planning,

00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:06.600
centralized logging, and segmentation between production areas and higher-level networks.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.650
Service accounts and vendor tools deserve particular attention because they may have broad

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:11.780
and persistent access.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.310
Level 3 is also a natural place to coordinate monitoring and response.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.820
It can provide context about production schedules, engineering changes,

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.020
and expected traffic.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.660
During an incident, the organization must understand which site services can be isolated,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.320
which are required for safe operation,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.820
and how lower-level control can continue if site coordination is degraded.
